// Constants: `NAN` — core's one const (f64 bits, materialized with
// `ConstRaw`), name-explicit like every other prelude name:
// `use core::{NAN}`. The float constants (`PI`, `E`, `INFINITY`, …)
// stay `calc`'s.

// ---- engine contracts ----
// The engine's contracts are SPELLINGS ON TYPES now (no `builtin
// trait` rows): the bracket markers `[disposal]` and `[iterable]`
// designate an inherent impl member (one per contract per class, the
// signature checked against the contract), and the closed `builtin
// class` pair below carries the async protocol. Library contracts
// without engine knowledge (a package's own trait — e.g. an encoding
// contract for map keys) stay plain `trait`.

// The iteration protocol. A type is iterable when an inherent impl
// marks a member `[iterable]`; `for (x of it)` calls that ONE
// designated member — `it.<member>(emit)` — the loop variable is ONE
// variable reassigned per iteration, and captures follow the
// language's capture law (scalars copy; ref-headed bindings share
// their slot): `emit` returning `false` stops the iteration (a
// `break`). The contract's shape: `fn <free>(self, emit: fn(E) ->
// bool)` — the element type E falls out of the marked member's own
// signature, and the member's name is free (the bracket designates).
// The builtin sequences (`[T]`, `Vec`, `str`, `bytes`) keep their
// fused loops and never mark a member.
//
//   impl Rows {
//       [iterable] pub fn iterate(self, emit: fn(Row) -> bool) { .. }
//   }
//   for (let r of rows) { .. }

// The cell-death contract. An inherent impl marks a member
// `[disposal]`; the engine calls it when a value of the type reaches
// refcount zero (weak boxes null first, fields release after it
// returns) at the next call boundary. The contract's shape: `fn
// <free>(mut self, cx: DisposalContext)` — the NAME IS FREE
// (designation by bracket, never by spelling), `mut self` first, the
// engine-minted context second, nil answered.
//
//   impl Db {
//       [disposal] fn dispose_db(mut self, cx: DisposalContext) { .. }
//   }

// The async protocol, the Future-only vocabulary — TWO CLOSED builtin
// classes (v20: the `builtin trait` pair is gone). Both are `pub
// builtin` (the import-gated spelling): the ENGINE weaves without
// them — every async producer's frame mints keyed on the engine
// symbols, never on user-scope resolution. Source that SPELLS the
// names resolves them only through `use core::{ Future }` / `use
// core::{ RunContext }`: a launcher's `f: Future<T>` parameter,
// `downcast<Future<..>>`, a `cx` probe in an async body.
//
// `Future<T>` — a cold, once-drivable computation, and the ONE type
// every async producer answers: an `async fn` call, an `async { }`
// block, `sleep`, `select2`, `completer` — all `Future<..>`, so
// `await`/`select2`/`launch_future` never ask "which kind". CLOSED:
// no constructor, no impl lane, no user-callable members — a user
// type cannot BE a Future (the walls are the point: structural
// satisfaction cannot see engine identity, so nominal closure is the
// only spelling of "engine-minted only"). The hidden frame is
// representation; the handle is the surface.
//
// `async fn` is genuine sugar over the async BLOCK — both sides are
// writable programs:
//
//     async fn work() -> nil { BODY }
//     // ≡
//     fn work() -> Future<nil> {
//         return async { BODY };
//     }
//
// `async { .. }` is the primitive: one expression form, evaluated
// where it stands (the mint is pure — the frame runs when driven).
// Its captures read by value at the mint (the capture law); its
// `return` answers the FUTURE, not any enclosing fn.
//
// `RunContext` — the cx protocol: the weave INJECTS the binding `cx`
// into every async body (an `async fn`'s parameters are ordinary
// values — a spelled cx is gone). `checkpoint()` reads this frame's
// resume state, `cancelled()` reads the task's abort flag. The
// members lower as inline field ops on the engine-minted record —
// the frozen surface is this signature set, nothing more.
//
// `launch_future` / `LaunchedFutureHandle` / `sleep` are HOST surface
// (the embedder's standard set), never core: users may write their own
// launchers over this same `Future` surface.
pub builtin class Future<T> {
}

pub builtin class RunContext {
    fn checkpoint(self) -> u32;
    fn cancelled(self) -> bool;
}

// engine-minted, one per `[disposal]` call; no members — the
// parameter exists so the context grows without touching the
// marker's signature
pub builtin class DisposalContext {
}
